[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What kinds of validation rules does the shared validator need to cover? → Required + common types[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Second question — this one changes what the module is allowed to touch, so here's the fork before I ask:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the shared piece is pure logic that hands back results, or also owns putting error text on the page.

  - [1mPure validator (returns errors)[0m — buys a module that's trivially testable with no DOM and reusable on a server too;
    costs each form writing its own few lines of "show the message." Cheap to change later: you can add a display helper
    on top without touching the validator.
  - [1mValidator + display helper, separate[0m — same pure core, plus an opt-in helper that renders messages next to fields.
    Buys consistent error UI across forms; costs a DOM contract (where the message element lives) that forms must
    follow. Still cheap to back out of, since the core stays pure.
  - [1mOne bind-to-form API[0m — [38;5;153mbindValidation(formEl, schema)[39m wires submit, validation, and display in one call. Buys the
    least per-form code; costs testability (needs a DOM), and it's the expensive one to reverse, because every form's
    markup and call site is built against it.

  [1mMy recommendation: the middle one.[0m Your current form logs errors to the console and shows the user nothing, so real
  forms will need display code regardless — but keeping it a separate layer over a pure core means the validation rules
  stay testable without a browser, which matters more here given there's no test setup yet.
